// @emit-error assignment target is not a parameter, local, or global
// THE OTHER SOUNDNESS BOUNDARY of the captured ref-array slot: READING a captured ref array
// works, ASSIGNING INTO one is still a loud reject.
//
// That floor is REP-INDEPENDENT — `emitAssign` resolves a target through the local / param /
// global classes only, so a captured `i32[]` and a captured `string[]` reject with this same
// message. It is worth pinning HERE because the message MOVED: on master this program stopped
// at `emitProgram: ref valtype with no interned shape` (the env field had no shape, so
// emission never reached the assignment), and the ref-list slot now carries far enough for the
// real floor to be the one the programmer is told about. The workaround is to return the new
// value and assign in the OWNING frame.

function body() {
  let xs: i32[][] = [[9, 8]]
  function inAssign() {
    xs = [[7]]
    print(xs[0][0])
  }
  inAssign()
}
body()
